\chapter{Keeping FBF data between runs}
\label{Cache}

Every run of a command used to start from nothing: a launch, then every
request again.
For one student in [[canvaslms submissions view]] (\cref{View}) that came
to some 40 requests and 7 to 12 seconds, of which the launch alone, four
hops one after the other, took more than two.
Most of it is the same as last time: the activity's tasks, the criteria of
a review, the participants and their addresses, the files handed in.
This chapter keeps that between runs, in two places: the data in an
encrypted cache file, the tokens in the system keyring.

The data does not all age alike, and the cache is built on that.
\begin{description}
\item[Lists] say what there is now: the participants' progresses, the work
  handed in, the review allocations, the reflections, the posts.
  They change while the students work, and a teacher looks at them to see
  what is new.
  We keep them for five minutes, which makes a run over many submissions,
  or one right after another, cheap, and leaves a view almost always
  fresh.
\item[Details] are what the lists point to: the tasks of an activity, the
  criteria and their levels, a user's name and addresses, a file.
  They hardly change, so we keep them for as long as [[canvaslms]] keeps
  its own objects, a week by default ([[DEFAULT_CACHE_TTL_DAYS]]).
\item[Validated details] are the reviews and ratings of an allocation.
  They do change, but the allocation, which is in a list, says how many
  there are and when they were last published.
  So a detail is used only while the counts and publication times it was
  fetched with are still those of the fresh allocation, which is at most
  five minutes old; when they differ, the list is fetched again at once,
  without waiting a week.
  Whether FBF changes those attributes when a review is edited we have not
  seen; an edit that changes neither waits for the week or for
  [[--refresh-cache]].
  The reviewee's feedback on a review has no such count on the
  allocation, so it is a list, kept five minutes.
\end{description}
What is in none of these classes is not cached at all.
That is the safe default for an endpoint we add later and forget to
classify: it costs time, not correctness.

The cache sits under the client's [[get]] (\cref{API}), so every command
gains, not only the view: [[fbf reviews]] asks for some 800 lists per
activity (\cref{Reviews}), which a second run within a week mostly takes
from the cache.

